# 1g/orphan — an INHERENT `impl` on a type from another module, which must be refused.
#
# The orphan rule says an `impl` belongs to a module that declared something it names: a spec
# impl may live with its spec or with its type, and an inherent one names only the type — so it
# belongs with the type and nowhere else. This is that rule with one of its two owners missing.
#
# IT WAS UNREACHABLE UNTIL A DOTTED TARGET WAS BUILT. While `impl shape.P { … }` was refused by
# name, the only `impl` a module could write was on a type it declared itself, so the rule had
# nothing to catch and no case could be written for it.
import "./shape"

impl shape.P {
	fn twice() -> int {
		return this.x * 2
	}
}

fn main() {
	print shape.P(3).twice()
}
